"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"][10]={"school_physics_en:M10-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M10-L1 – Electromagnets and electric motors<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Electromagnetism and electricity supply<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Why does a battery-powered model motor spin, whereas a coil with no\ncurrent does not keep speeding up?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>An <strong>electromagnet<\/strong> is produced when current flows\nthrough a coil. Its magnetic field depends on the direction of current.\nA soft-iron core can strengthen its magnetic effect. Switching off the\ncurrent largely removes the field generated by the coil, although some\nmaterials can retain magnetisation.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>current-carrying conductor in an external magnetic\nfield<\/strong> may experience a force. In a suitable arrangement, the\nforce is perpendicular to the current direction and the magnetic field.\nOn opposite sides of a rotating coil, forces may act in opposite\ndirections and together produce a <strong>turning effect\n(torque)<\/strong>. As an extension, for a straight wire perpendicular to\na uniform field the force magnitude is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>I<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>l<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=B\\,I\\,l<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis magnetic flux density in tesla,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis current, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>l<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe effective wire length.<\/p>\n<p>A simple DC motor uses a <strong>commutator<\/strong> to reverse\ncurrent in the rotor coil after each half-turn, allowing the driving\ntorque to keep approximately the same direction through most of the\ncycle. Other motor designs use electronic commutation or alternating\ncurrent. Input electrical energy becomes useful mechanical energy, with\nsome transfer into internal energy and sound.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching graphic:<\/strong> Two opposing forces\nacting on wire segments in a magnetic field, together with an\nelectrical-input-to-rotation-and-losses energy chain. This is not a\nbuildable wiring diagram.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_058_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A magnetic field points right between N and S. Currents into and out of the page experience opposite forces; an energy-flow diagram is shown alongside.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A magnetic field points right between N\nand S. Currents into and out of the page experience opposite forces; an\nenergy-flow diagram is shown alongside.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Forces on opposite coil segments can cause rotation; electric\nmotors convert electrical energy to mechanical motion.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>Consider a constructed example:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=0.20\\,\\mathrm T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=0.50\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l=0.10\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a straight wire perpendicular to the field. The magnitude is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.010<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=BIl=0.20\\cdot0.50\\cdot0.10\\,\\mathrm N=0.010\\,\\mathrm N.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This force alone does not specify the torque of a real motor. The\nlever arm, number of active wire segments, rotor orientation and field\ndistribution would also matter.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe demonstration:<\/strong> Under teacher supervision, use a\nready-made classroom extra-low-voltage motor to observe the direction of\nrotation with permitted supply polarity. Disconnect the supply before\nchanging connections, avoid stalling the motor, use current limiting,\nand follow the equipment ratings. Alternatively, predict the motion of\nthe model loop from the diagram.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can a current-carrying coil behave as an electromagnet?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What useful energy output does an electric motor mainly\nprovide?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find F for B = 0.30 T, I = 0.40 A and l = 0.20 m at right\nangles.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does a basic DC motor need appropriate current reversal?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Electric current creates a magnetic field; a coil concentrates the\nmagnetic effect.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Mechanical motion or useful mechanical work.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">F = 0.30 × 0.40 × 0.20 N = 0.024 N.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">To prevent the driving torque from reversing its sustained direction\nafter the next half-turn.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The stated F = BIl formula assumes a perpendicular wire in a nearly\nuniform field. Real motors have feedback and counter-voltage. Motors and\ngenerators use related magnetic interactions but opposite useful\ndirections of energy conversion.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L2<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Server – Physics: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Curriculum content\non electromagnetism, induction, and technological energy transfer · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/kraft-auf-stromleiter-e-motor/grundwissen/elektromotor\">LEIFIphysik\n– Electric motor<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Magnetic action on the rotor and the function of\ncommutation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/induktion-und-transformator/grundwissen/generator-und-motorprinzip\">LEIFIphysik\n– Generator and motor principles<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Comparison of motor and generator\nenergy conversion · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/physik/praktisches-arbeiten/elektrik\">DGUV\nSafe School – Electricity experiments<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Rules for isolated classroom\nlow-voltage equipment and de-energised rearrangements · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M10-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M10-L2 – Changing magnetic flux and induction<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Electromagnetism and electricity supply<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A coil is connected to a sensitive voltmeter. A bar magnet rests\ninside it and the pointer is at zero. Why does the pointer move when the\nmagnet moves?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>An <strong>induced voltage<\/strong> can arise whenever\n<strong>magnetic flux<\/strong> through a conducting loop changes with\ntime. This can happen when magnet or coil moves, the magnetic field\nstrength changes, or the loop’s effective area or orientation changes.\nFor a flat surface in a uniform field,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Phi=B A\\cos\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the angle between the field and the surface normal.<\/p>\n<p>For a coil with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsimilar turns, an extension formula for the average induced voltage\nis<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(average voltage over the interval).<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_{\\mathrm{ind}}=-N\\frac{\\Delta\\Phi}{\\Delta t}\\quad\\text{(average voltage over the interval).}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The minus sign expresses <strong>Lenz’s law<\/strong>: when a circuit\nis closed, the induced current produces a magnetic effect opposing the\n<em>change<\/em> that caused it. An open circuit can have a measurable\nvoltage without a sustained conduction current. A strong but\n<strong>unchanging<\/strong> flux is not enough.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching graphic:<\/strong> A magnet moves towards or\naway from a coil, with opposite deflections of a simplified voltage\npointer. With the magnet stationary, the pointer returns to zero.\nDirections refer to the marked orientation.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_059_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three model states: magnet approaching a coil with positive deflection, stationary with zero, then moving away with reverse deflection.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three model states: magnet approaching a\ncoil with positive deflection, stationary with zero, then moving away\nwith reverse deflection.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>What matters is the change in magnetic flux; polarity is defined\nby the fixed meter terminals.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose flux per turn changes from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.010<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.010\\,\\mathrm{Wb}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.004<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.004\\,\\mathrm{Wb}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.20\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nin a model coil with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe <strong>magnitude<\/strong> of mean induced voltage is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">¯<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0.006<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><mrow><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\overline{U}_{\\mathrm{ind}}|=N\\frac{|\\Delta\\Phi|}{\\Delta t}=100\\cdot\\frac{0.006\\,\\mathrm{Wb}}{0.20\\,\\mathrm s}=3.0\\,\\mathrm V.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Assigning its sign requires specifying loop direction and meter\nterminals. These are constructed example values.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe classroom experiment:<\/strong> Connect a small coil to\nan approved high-impedance meter and move a suitable bar magnet in and\nout. Compare the pointer at rest, on approach, on withdrawal, and at\ndifferent speeds. Avoid uncontrolled strong neodymium magnets; consider\npinch risks, implanted devices and nearby electronics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does constant magnetic flux through a stationary loop induce a\nsustained voltage?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Give two ways of changing flux through a loop.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find |Uind| for N = 50, |ΔΦ| = 0.004 Wb and Δt = 0.10 s.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does Lenz’s law tell us?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No in the stationary model; without a changing flux the induced\nvoltage is zero.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For example, move a magnet or rotate the coil; changing field\nstrength also works.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">50 × 0.004 / 0.10 V = 2.0 V.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The induced current acts to oppose the magnetic-flux change that\ncauses it.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>A finite-difference expression gives the average induced voltage.\nInstantaneous voltage depends on the instantaneous rate of change. The\npointer sketch does not specify every sign convention.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L3<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Server – Physics: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Curriculum content\non electromagnetism, induction, and technological energy transfer · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/induktion-generator-und-transformator/grundwissen/auftreten-von-induktion\">LEIFIphysik\n– Occurrence of induction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Coil, magnet, meter response and\ndependence on magnetic-flux change · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/physik/praktisches-arbeiten/elektrik\">DGUV\nSafe School – Electricity experiments<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Rules for isolated classroom\nlow-voltage equipment and de-energised rearrangements · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M10-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M10-L3 – Generator principle and alternating voltage<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Electromagnetism and electricity supply<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Why does a hand-driven bicycle generator produce voltage readings\nthat change, and why does the electrical effect often grow at faster\nrotation?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>generator<\/strong> receives mechanical work through\nrotation. As a conducting loop turns relative to a magnetic field, the\nmagnetic flux through it changes and an induced voltage appears across\nits terminals. For a uniformly rotating ideal loop in a constant field,\nvoltage can be <strong>sinusoidal<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>T<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(t)=\\hat U\\sin(2\\pi f t+\\varphi_0),\\qquad f=\\frac1T.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis peak voltage,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrequency and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nperiod. An <strong>alternating voltage<\/strong> takes both positive and\nnegative values relative to fixed terminal labels. Real generator\nwaveforms and peak values depend on construction and load. Electrical\noutput does not come free: when a generator supplies more power,\nadditional mechanical work is required, often felt as greater resistance\nto turning.<\/p>\n<p>A generator does not create energy from nothing: <strong>mechanical\ninput → useful electrical output + unavoidable losses<\/strong>. Bicycle\ngenerators, wind turbines and larger turbine-driven generators share\nthis general conversion idea, although their designs differ.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching graphic:<\/strong> A rotating loop in a\nsimplified magnetic field and an alternating-voltage trace below it,\nshowing positive and negative half-cycles.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_060_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic rotating coil between magnetic poles and a voltage–time curve alternating above and below zero.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic rotating coil between magnetic\npoles and a voltage–time curve alternating above and below\nzero.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>With uniform rotation under the model assumptions, induced\nvoltage changes sign periodically.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>An idealised trace repeats after\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.040<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=0.040\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts frequency is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>T<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>0.040<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=\\frac1T=\\frac1{0.040\\,\\mathrm s}=25\\,\\mathrm{Hz}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat U=4.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe ideal extreme instantaneous values are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>4.0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+4.0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-4.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese values are <strong>not<\/strong> the RMS voltage. For a pure sine\nwave, the extension relation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_\\mathrm{rms}=\\hat U/\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe demonstration:<\/strong> Use a ready-made protected hand\ngenerator and a suitable extra-low-voltage measuring device, turning the\nhandle slowly. Where the instrument is approved, display voltage against\ntime. Never connect a classroom hand generator to mains or unapproved\nelectronics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What useful input form of energy drives a generator?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does a negative alternating-voltage value mean?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find f when T = 0.020 s.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does a loaded generator usually become harder to turn?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Mechanical energy or mechanical work.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The instantaneous polarity has reversed relative to the chosen\nreference terminals.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">f = 1/0.020 s = 50 Hz.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Energy conservation requires additional mechanical work to provide\nelectrical output.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The sine curve assumes uniform rotation and an appropriate idealised\nfield. A simple commutator can produce pulsating DC instead of a signed\nalternating voltage. Neither frequency nor peak voltage alone describes\nthe capability of a real generator.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L4<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Server – Physics: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Curriculum content\non electromagnetism, induction, and technological energy transfer · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/induktion-und-transformator/versuche/generator\">LEIFIphysik\n– Generator experiment<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Rotating coil, alternating-voltage trace\nand commutator behaviour · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/induktion-und-transformator/grundwissen/generator-und-motorprinzip\">LEIFIphysik\n– Generator and motor principles<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Comparison of motor and generator\nenergy conversion · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/physik/praktisches-arbeiten/elektrik\">DGUV\nSafe School – Electricity experiments<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Rules for isolated classroom\nlow-voltage equipment and de-energised rearrangements · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M10-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M10-L4 – Transformers and energy transfer<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Electromagnetism and electricity supply<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A classroom transformer has 200 turns on its primary coil and 50 on\nits secondary. How does an approved AC supply affect the secondary\nvoltage?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>transformer<\/strong> has two electrically separated\nwindings sharing a magnetic core. Changing current in the primary\ncreates changing magnetic flux, which induces voltage in the secondary.\nAn ordinary fixed-core transformer therefore does <strong>not<\/strong>\noperate continuously from steady DC; after switch-on a constant primary\ncurrent creates no continuing flux change.<\/p>\n<p>For an <strong>ideal transformer<\/strong>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{U_2}{U_1}=\\frac{N_2}{N_1},\\qquad U_1 I_1=U_2 I_2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_1,N_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare turns counts. Voltages are RMS values for sinusoidal operation, or\ncorresponding consistent AC quantities. Ideal conservation of\ntransferred power produces an inverse current ratio. <strong>Stepping up\nvoltage does not create additional energy<\/strong>: for equal power,\ncurrent falls. Ohmic line losses may be approximated by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_\\mathrm{loss}=I^2R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is one reason appropriately designed transmission systems use high\nvoltages, always with strict engineering safeguards.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching graphic:<\/strong> Two separate windings\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_1=200<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_2=50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na simple shared core, and voltage labels. It is not a mains installation\nguide.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_061_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Electrically separate primary and secondary windings on a shared core, with N1 = 200, N2 = 50, U1 = 12 V, U2 = 3 V in an ideal example.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Electrically separate primary and\nsecondary windings on a shared core, with N1 = 200, N2 = 50, U1 = 12 V,\nU2 = 3 V in an ideal example.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>In the ideal transformer voltage scales with turns count; primary\nand secondary are electrically separated.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>Consider an <strong>ideal<\/strong> educational transformer with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_1=200<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_2=50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand an approved isolated AC input of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_1=12.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mfrac><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mfrac><mn>50<\/mn><mn>200<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_2=U_1\\frac{N_2}{N_1}=12.0\\,\\mathrm V\\cdot\\frac{50}{200}=3.0\\,\\mathrm V.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If the secondary provides\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_2=0.80\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_2=2.4\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand ideal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_1=P_1/U_1=0.20\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nReal losses require slightly more input power.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Approved classroom transformer only:<\/strong> A teacher may\ndemonstrate a suitable unit using properly isolated extra-low-voltage AC\nand rated meters. Pupils must never wire transformers to wall outlets or\nperform short-circuit tests without a formal safety assessment. A\npaper-only comparison of turns ratios is a safer alternative.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must linked magnetic flux change in an ordinary\ntransformer?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find U2 for U1 = 8 V, N1 = 100 and N2 = 300 (ideal).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At equal power, what happens to current if secondary voltage\nincreases?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can high transmission voltage reduce line losses?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Changing flux is needed to sustain induced secondary voltage.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">U2 = 8 V × 300/100 = 24 V.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Current decreases because P = UI stays equal in the ideal\nmodel.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For equal power, the current is lower and resistive losses fall with\nI².<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The ratios assume ideal magnetic coupling. Core losses, winding\nresistance and loading affect real values. This explanation is not\nsufficient to design or verify a mains-connected transformer.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L5<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Server – Physics: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Curriculum content\non electromagnetism, induction, and technological energy transfer · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/induktion-generator-und-transformator/grundwissen/transformator\">LEIFIphysik\n– Transformer<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Changing magnetic field, secondary induction and\nabsence of steady-DC operation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/physik/praktisches-arbeiten/elektrik\">DGUV\nSafe School – Electricity experiments<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Rules for isolated classroom\nlow-voltage equipment and de-energised rearrangements · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M10-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M10-L5 – Electricity grids, supply and storage<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Electromagnetism and electricity supply<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Where can energy from a wind turbine go when immediate electricity\ndemand is lower than the turbine could supply?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>Electricity supply is a time-dependent chain of <strong>conversion –\ntransmission – distribution – use<\/strong>. Wind and hydropower convert\nmotion; photovoltaics convert radiation directly into electrical energy.\nMany large generating stations drive generators with turbines. Grids\ntransmit electrical energy through AC systems; different voltage levels\nand transformers allow practical, efficient transmission.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Storage<\/strong> absorbs energy in one form and releases some\nlater. Examples include pumped hydro (gravitational potential energy)\nand batteries (chemical energy). Storage <strong>does not\ncreate<\/strong> energy and causes charging, discharging and standby\nlosses. Flexible demand, interconnections, controllable generation and\nstorage all help balance power production and consumption in real\ntime.<\/p>\n<p>For a simple storage model, round-trip efficiency is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=E_\\mathrm{out}/E_\\mathrm{in}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le\\eta\\le1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEnergy can be expressed in joules or kilowatt-hours:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{kWh}=3.6\\,\\mathrm{MJ}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Power<\/strong> in kW describes a rate; storage capacity in kWh\ndescribes an amount of energy.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching graphic:<\/strong> Energy flow from a wind\ngenerator through a transformer and grid to consumers, with a branch to\na battery icon and an explicitly marked loss arrow.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_062_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Flow chart from wind turbine through transformer and grid to consumers with a storage branch and explicit energy losses.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Flow chart from wind turbine through\ntransformer and grid to consumers with a storage branch and explicit\nenergy losses.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Electricity supply connects conversion, transmission, use and\ntime-shifted storage, including losses.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A model store receives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\mathrm{in}=10\\,\\mathrm{kWh}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand later delivers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\mathrm{out}=8\\,\\mathrm{kWh}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>8<\/mn><mn>10<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=\\frac{8}{10}=0.80=80\\,\\%.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The difference is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{kWh}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>7.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7.2\\,\\mathrm{MJ}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nin real conversions some is transferred as internal energy. A steady\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{kW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nload could use the available\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8\\,\\mathrm{kWh}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor four hours in a simplified constant-power model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Paper exercise:<\/strong> Put hourly demand data alongside a\nconstructed wind-power profile and mark when a model store charges or\ndischarges. Never connect a classroom demonstration to household\nelectrical installations. Approved school extra-low-voltage equipment\nonly for physical models.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name two physically different forms of energy storage.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does a large installed generator capacity not guarantee supply\nat every moment?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find efficiency for 12 kWh stored and 9 kWh returned.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the difference between kW and kWh?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Pumped storage (gravitational potential energy) and a battery\n(chemical energy), for example.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Weather, availability and demand vary; generation and consumption\nmust be balanced.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">η = 9/12 = 0.75 = 75 %.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">kW is power (energy per unit time); kWh is energy.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The graphic is an energy-path model, not a description of real-time\ngrid frequency control, reactive power, multiple storage cycles or\nelectricity markets. Numerical storage values are invented exercises,\nnot product claims.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L6<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Server – Physics: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Curriculum content\non electromagnetism, induction, and technological energy transfer · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.smard.de/page/home/wiki-article/446/396\">German\nfederal energy data portal SMARD – Balancing reserves<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Requirement\nto balance physical electricity generation and demand · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.umweltbundesamt.de/daten/umweltzustand-trends/energie/erneuerbare-konventionelle-stromerzeugung\">German\nEnvironment Agency – Renewable and conventional electricity\ngeneration<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Electricity generation types and evolution of the\ngeneration mix · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/physik/praktisches-arbeiten/elektrik\">DGUV\nSafe School – Electricity experiments<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Rules for isolated classroom\nlow-voltage equipment and de-energised rearrangements · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M10-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M10-L6 – Evaluating electricity sources<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Electromagnetism and electricity supply<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Two power stations can deliver the same electrical power. Do they\nnecessarily have identical effects on climate, nature and supply\nsecurity?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>Comparing <strong>electricity sources<\/strong> requires multiple\ncriteria. <strong>Wind<\/strong> exploits the motion of air,\n<strong>hydropower<\/strong> gravitational and kinetic energy of water,\nand <strong>photovoltaics<\/strong> solar radiation. <strong>Coal and\nnatural gas<\/strong> convert chemical energy to heat and often via a\nturbine and generator into electricity. <strong>Nuclear plants<\/strong>\nobtain heat from fission and commonly drive a turbine-generator system\nas well.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Power is not energy:<\/strong> A plant rated at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_\\mathrm{rated}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes not produce at that level continuously. Over a period,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=\\int P(t)\\,dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor a model with a constant average power,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=P_\\mathrm{avg}t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA <strong>capacity factor<\/strong> over a stated period is actual energy\ndivided by the energy that continuous rated output would have produced.\nValues depend on location, technology and the period considered.<\/p>\n<p>Environmental assessment has <strong>several dimensions<\/strong>:\ngreenhouse gases in operation and across the life cycle, material needs,\nland use, ecosystems, waste, water use and safety and reliability\ncriteria. Fossil-fuel combustion releases CO₂ and other air pollutants.\nWind and photovoltaics have no combustion emissions while operating, but\ninfrastructure and manufacturing have impacts. Nuclear generation has\nlow direct operational CO₂ emissions yet specific waste and risk issues.\nThe importance attached to different criteria is a societal judgement,\nnot settled by one physics calculation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching graphic:<\/strong> A neutral comparison grid\nfor four technology groups showing physical input, dispatchability and\nenvironmental considerations, deliberately avoiding an overall\nranking.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_063_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Comparison grid for wind, photovoltaics, fossil thermal and nuclear energy, organised by physical energy source, timing and impacts.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Comparison grid for wind, photovoltaics,\nfossil thermal and nuclear energy, organised by physical energy source,\ntiming and impacts.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Neutral comparison distinguishes physical conversion,\navailability and different environmental effects.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>fictional<\/strong> wind turbine rated at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.0\\,\\mathrm{MW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngenerates\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\,\\mathrm{MWh}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nover a 24-hour interval. Continuous rated operation would produce\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>48<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">48\\,\\mathrm{MWh}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe capacity factor for this particular modeled day is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>12<\/mn><mn>48<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=\\frac{12\\,\\mathrm{MWh}}{2.0\\,\\mathrm{MW}\\cdot24\\,\\mathrm h}=\\frac{12}{48}=25\\,\\%.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This is a <strong>single-day model value<\/strong>, not a real plant’s\nannual statistic or a climate-impact figure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Source comparison on paper:<\/strong> Choose three criteria\nfor wind, photovoltaic and fossil combustion. Identify which quantities\ncan be measured, which need studies and which involve values or policy\nchoices. State the assessment method. No hazardous power, combustion or\nnuclear-material experiment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is rated power not the same thing as generated daily\nenergy?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name two environmental effects other than greenhouse gases.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A 1 MW generator delivers 6 MWh in 12 h. Find the model capacity\nfactor.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does physics alone not determine a complete policy ranking of\nall energy technologies?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Power is an instantaneous or reference rate; energy depends on the\nentire time profile of power.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For example, land use, raw materials, waste or effects on\nwaterways.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">c = 6 MWh/(1 MW × 12 h) = 0.50 = 50 %.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Physics supplies testable evidence, but weighting environment,\nsafety, cost and security requires additional explicit value\njudgements.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>This lesson does not assert present-day prices, actual plant capacity\nfactors or site-specific emissions. Real decisions require current data\nwith a clear publication date, method, reference year and system\nboundary. Renewable does not mean impact-free; dispatchable does not\nmean climate-neutral.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Looking ahead:<\/strong> The next module begins with <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L1 – The electromagnetic\nspectrum and wavelength<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Server – Physics: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Curriculum content\non electromagnetism, induction, and technological energy transfer · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.umweltbundesamt.de/daten/umweltzustand-trends/energie/erneuerbare-konventionelle-stromerzeugung\">German\nEnvironment Agency – Renewable and conventional electricity\ngeneration<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Electricity generation types and evolution of the\ngeneration mix · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.umweltbundesamt.de/daten/umweltzustand-trends/energie/energiebedingte-emissionen-von-klimagasen\">German\nEnvironment Agency – Energy-related emissions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Greenhouse gases and\nair pollution caused by fossil combustion · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.umweltbundesamt.de/indikator-umweltkosten-von-energie-strassenverkehr\">German\nEnvironment Agency – Environmental costs of energy<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Environmental\neffects and assessment limits of electricity production · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
